,"blocks":[{"type":"columns","cols":[{"width":50,"background":"var( --e-global-color-d077a13 )","padding":"30px 0px 30px 0px","blocks":[{"type":"heading","level":1,"text":"Truck Wrap Installation: What to Expect from Drop-Off to Pickup","style":"text-align:center;font-family:\"Poppins\", Sans-serif;font-size:35px;line-height:1.2em;color:#FFFFFF","box":"padding:0px 20px 0px 20px"}]},{"width":50,"blocks":[{"type":"image","src":"/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Hungarock-Chevy-Silverado-Full-Van-Wrap-by-Vinyl-Wrap-Toronto-Wrapping-Process.webp","alt":"Chevy Silverado part way through a full truck wrap installation at Vinyl Wrap Toronto, with printed panels being applied","full":true,"width":1200,"height":630}]}]}]},{"id":null,"background":"#FFFFFF","padding":"0px 0px 40px 0px","blocks":[{"type":"columns","cols":[{"width":74.87,"blocks":[{"type":"text","html":"<p>Most owners see two moments of a truck wrap: the day they drop the truck off and the day they pick it up looking completely different. Everything in between is a mystery, which is why the most common questions we hear are \"how long does it take to wrap a truck?\" and \"what do you actually do to it?\" This guide walks through a <strong>truck wrap installation</strong> from the first conversation to the first wash, so you know what happens at each stage, what the shop needs from you, and why a proper truck wrap installation takes the time it takes.</p>\n<p>The steps below describe the truck wrap process for a printed commercial wrap on a pickup, cube van or work truck, which is the most common job in a Toronto wrap shop. A colour-change truck wrap installation follows the same path without the design and printing stages.</p>\n\n<h2>Before the Truck Wrap Installation: Design, Proofing and Printing</h2>\n<p>The truck wrap installation itself is the last third of the job. A good result is mostly decided before any film touches the truck.</p>\n<h3>Measurements and photos</h3>\n<p>The designer works from a vehicle template that matches your exact make, model, cab and box length, then checks it against photos of your truck. Racks, toolboxes, tonneau covers, side steps and body damage all change which panels are usable, so the shop will want recent photos of the truck as it is now, not the brochure version. Our guide on <a href=\"/how-to-take-vehicle-photos-for-ai-wrap-design/\">taking useful vehicle photos</a> shows the angles that help.</p>\n<h3>The proof</h3>\n<p>You will receive a flat proof showing the design laid over the template, usually from the driver side, passenger side, rear and front. This is the moment to check every phone number, website address and spelling, because once the film is printed, a correction means reprinting that panel. At Vinyl Wrap Toronto the first design concept is usually ready within 48 hours of receiving your logo and details, and installation is scheduled once you have approved the final proof. A vector logo file speeds this up more than anything else, as our article on <a href=\"/best-vehicle-wrap-file-types/\">vehicle wrap file types</a> explains.</p>\n<h3>Printing, drying and laminating</h3>\n<p>Printed wraps are produced on a wide-format printer, then left to dry so the solvents in the ink can escape from the film. Film manufacturers specify this drying period, and skipping it is one of the classic causes of a wrap that lifts or bubbles later, because trapped solvent weakens the adhesive. After drying, a clear laminate is applied over the print to protect it from UV, fuel and scratches. Our post on <a href=\"/vehicle-wrap-laminate-printed-wraps/\">vehicle wrap laminate</a> covers why this layer is not optional on a work truck.</p>\n<p>Because the printed panels need to be ready before the truck arrives, this stage is why a shop asks for your approval a few days before your truck wrap installation date rather than the morning of.</p>\n\n<h2>Drop-Off Day: What the Shop Needs from You</h2>\n<p>Arrive with the truck washed, empty of loose cargo, and with any aftermarket accessories you want kept out of the wrap area already discussed. The cleaner the truck arrives, the sooner the installers can start on the work that matters. If you have not yet read it, our <a href=\"/prepare-your-truck-for-a-wrap/\">truck preparation checklist</a> lists everything worth doing the week before.</p>\n<p>Expect a walk-around with the installer before the truck goes into the bay. This is a pre-installation inspection, and 3M''s <a href=\"https://multimedia.3m.com/mws/media/2419563O/application-vehicles-ra-pdf.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">vehicle application guide</a> recommends installers complete and sign one before every vehicle graphic job. The installer is looking for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Paint condition.</strong> Repainted panels, touch-ups, peeling clear coat, rust and body filler are all noted. Film sticks to whatever the top layer is, so if that layer is loose, the wrap can pull it away during removal.</li>\n<li><strong>Dents, scratches and existing damage.</strong> These are documented so both sides agree on the truck''s condition going in, and so the installer can plan around dents that film cannot hide.</li>\n<li><strong>Areas where film tends to lift.</strong> Deep channels, the undersides of bumpers, wheel-well lips and seams between panels are flagged for extra attention or excluded from coverage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Be honest about repairs and repainting: an installer who knows a tailgate was resprayed last year can test adhesion in a corner first.</p>\n\n<h2>How Long Does It Take to Wrap a Truck?</h2>\n<p>There is no single answer, and any shop that quotes an exact number of hours before seeing the truck is guessing. Our <a href=\"/truck-wraps/\">truck wrap page</a> gives a range of roughly one to five days depending on the size and complexity of the truck, and the variables are easy to list:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Coverage.</strong> Lettering and decals can be done in hours. A <a href=\"/partial-truck-wrap-toronto-2/\">partial truck wrap</a> on the doors and box sides is usually a single day. A <a href=\"/full-truck-wrap-gta-toronto/\">full truck wrap</a> that covers the hood, roof, bumpers and every panel can run to several days.</li>\n<li><strong>Vehicle size and shape.</strong> A crew cab with a long box has far more surface than a regular cab. Cube vans and straight trucks have rivets and corrugated panels that each take time, as our <a href=\"/box-truck-wrap-guide-rivets-corrugations/\">box truck wrap guide</a> explains.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardware.</strong> Removing and reinstalling door handles, mirrors, lights, badges and trim adds time but produces cleaner edges than cutting around them.</li>\n<li><strong>Condition.</strong> A truck that arrives dirty, waxed or with accessories still bolted on adds hours before the first panel goes on.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Plan for the truck to be off the road for the whole truck wrap installation plus the settling time described below. For a working truck, that means booking the install for your quietest days rather than squeezing it between jobs.</p>\n\n<h2>Inside the Bay: The Truck Wrap Installation Step by Step</h2>\n<h3>1. Decontamination</h3>\n<p>Even a freshly washed truck is treated as contaminated at the start of a truck wrap installation. 3M''s application guidance calls for washing with a mild detergent solution, drying thoroughly including inside seams and panel gaps, then wiping every surface with a 70 percent isopropyl alcohol and water mixture immediately before the film is applied. Wax, sealants and the slick additives in some automatic car washes all reduce adhesion, so anything on the paint has to come off. If you have recently applied a ceramic coating or sealant, tell the shop; our article on <a href=\"/wrap-over-ppf-or-ceramic-coating/\">wrapping over PPF or a ceramic coating</a> explains why it matters.</p>\n<h3>2. Hardware removal</h3>\n<p>Depending on the coverage agreed, installers remove or loosen the parts that sit on top of the panels being wrapped: handles, mirror caps, badges, lights and trim pieces. Wrapping under these parts instead of around them is what separates a wrap with hidden edges from one with visible cut lines. Everything comes off in a sequence and goes back on the same way at the end.</p>\n<h3>3. A controlled environment</h3>\n<p>Film is applied indoors in a clean bay where the temperature can be controlled. 3M''s general guidance is that the air and the vehicle surface should both be between 16 and 32 degrees Celsius during application, and its 2080 colour-change films are rated for a narrower band around room temperature. Too cold and the film cannot be stretched into curves without cooling before it sets; too warm and the adhesive grabs before the installer can position the panel. This is a practical reason that wraps in Toronto are a shop job in winter, not a driveway job.</p>\n<h3>4. Panel-by-panel application</h3>\n<p>The wrap arrives as a set of printed panels, each sized to a section of the truck with extra margin for wrapping around edges. Installers position each panel, remove the liner, and work the film onto the surface with a squeegee in overlapping strokes so no air is left behind. Modern wrap films use an air-release adhesive, which lets the film be lifted and repositioned and lets trapped air escape through microscopic channels rather than forming bubbles. On curves, recesses and body lines the film is warmed with a heat gun so it can stretch and conform, then pressed into place before it cools.</p>\n<p>On a printed wrap the installer is also aligning the graphic across panel gaps, so a stripe that runs from the door to the box lines up when the door is closed. That alignment is checked constantly and is a large part of why skilled work takes the time it does.</p>\n<h3>5. Trimming at seams and edges</h3>\n<p>Film is cut at every panel seam, because body panels flex independently as the truck moves and film bridging a seam will tear or pull away. Edges are tucked under or trimmed just short of rubber gaskets and window seals. Around lights, handles and badges, installers may use knifeless cutting tape so no blade touches the paint, a technique we describe in our <a href=\"/knifeless-tape-car-wrap/\">knifeless tape article</a>.</p>\n<h3>6. Post-heating</h3>\n<p>Every area where the film was stretched, and every edge, is heated again after it is applied. This step, called post-heating, erases the film''s memory so it stops trying to shrink back to its original shape. 3M''s guidance is to bring stretched film to 93 to 107 degrees Celsius, measured on the film surface with an infrared thermometer, and to re-squeegee all edges and overlaps before the vehicle is released. An installer who skips post-heating has a wrap that looks perfect on pickup day and lifts at the recesses a few weeks later. Manufacturer-certified installers are trained on this step, which is part of what the certification in our <a href=\"/certified-vinyl-wrap-installer-3m-vs-avery/\">3M vs Avery certification article</a> actually covers.</p>\n<h3>7. Reassembly and inspection</h3>\n<p>Hardware goes back on, every edge is checked by hand, and the truck is inspected under good lighting for lifted edges, trapped debris or alignment issues. On a lettering job this is a quick check. On a full truck wrap installation with many removed parts it can take a few hours on its own, which is one more reason the finish time on a large truck is an estimate rather than a promise.</p>\n\n<figure><img src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Hungarock-Chevy-Silverado-Full-Vinyl-Wrap-Before-and-After.webp\" alt=\"Before and after a full truck wrap installation on a Chevy Silverado work truck\" width=\"1200\" height=\"630\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" /><figcaption>A Chevy Silverado before and after a full printed wrap. The printed panels, hardware removal and post-heating all happen between these two photos.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>After a Truck Wrap: The First 48 Hours and Beyond</h2>\n<p>The adhesive keeps building its bond after the truck leaves the shop, so the first days after a truck wrap installation matter. The two film makers we install give similar advice.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keep it warm and dry at first.</strong> 3M advises keeping the vehicle above 16 degrees Celsius for at least 12 hours after installation before exposing it to cold or wet conditions. In a Toronto winter, that means a heated garage or leaving the truck at the shop overnight rather than parking it on the street the same evening.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not wash it right away.</strong> 3M says no washing for at least 24 hours; Avery Dennison''s <a href=\"https://graphics.averydennison.com/content/dam/averydennison/graphics/eu/en/technical-bulletins/General/TB-1.6-Maintenance-and-Cleaning-of-Avery-decals-and-graphics.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">maintenance and cleaning bulletin</a> says no cleaning or washing for the first 48 hours. Follow the longer window and you are covered either way.</li>\n<li><strong>Hand wash, or touchless only.</strong> After the waiting period, hand washing with a mild car soap is the safest method. 3M permits a touchless automatic wash; brush washes can scuff the laminate and catch edges. Never aim a pressure washer at the edges of the film: Avery Dennison caps pressure washing at 80 bar, water below 70 degrees Celsius, with the nozzle at least 75 centimetres from the graphic and held square to the surface, and 3M warns that high-pressure spray at the edges causes lifting.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid solvents, abrasives and wax.</strong> Both manufacturers warn against solvent cleaners and abrasive products on wrap film. Our <a href=\"/top-8-maintenance-tips-for-vehicle-wraps/\">wrap maintenance tips</a> go into the products that are safe.</li>\n<li><strong>Look it over after the first week.</strong> Walk around the truck and check edges, recesses and seams. A small lifted corner is a quick fix if it is caught early and a much bigger problem once dirt gets underneath. Vinyl Wrap Toronto''s installs include a 3-year warranty against peeling, bubbling and fading, so report anything you see rather than pressing it down yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>What a Good Truck Wrap Installation Should Not Include</h2>\n<p>Knowing the proper truck wrap process also tells you what to question. Be wary of any shop that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Offers to wrap the truck outdoors or in an unheated space in cold weather.</li>\n<li>Prints and installs the same day, with no drying time for the film.</li>\n<li>Cuts around every handle and mirror rather than removing any hardware, without explaining why.</li>\n<li>Cannot tell you which film and laminate are being used, or whether the installer is manufacturer certified.</li>\n<li>Quotes a firm number of hours for a full wrap without seeing the truck.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of these guarantees a bad wrap, but each one removes a safeguard the film manufacturers consider standard. Our guide on <a href=\"/cheap-vinyl-wrap-hidden-costs/\">the hidden costs of cheap wraps</a> covers what usually goes wrong when corners are cut.</p>\n\n<h2>Ready to Book Your Truck Wrap Installation?</h2>\n<p>Once you know the steps, scheduling a truck wrap installation is simple: send us your logo and photos of the truck, approve the proof, and book the truck in for the number of days the coverage needs. Vinyl Wrap Toronto installs 3M and Avery Dennison film at our shop in Etobicoke, with certified installers and a 3-year warranty on the work. <a href=\"/contact/\">Request a free quote</a> or call <a href=\"tel:416-746-1381\">416-746-1381</a> to talk through coverage and timing for your truck, or browse our <a href=\"/commercial-vehicle-wraps/\">commercial vehicle wrap services</a> to see what each option involves.</p>\n","style":"text-align:start;font-family:\"Poppins\", Sans-serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.5em;font-weight:300"}]},{"width":25,"background":"#F8F8F8","padding":"10px 10px 10px 10px","blocks":[{"type":"toc","title":"Table of Contents","box":"background-color:#F8F8F8"},{"type":"form"}]}]}]}]', NULL, datetime('now')
